{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792488511,"nonce":"+pUzTXgj5RUVXf6gWS/AwbMWAEk=","message":"Landed in 4a9c7880f762c30e12c590a6dbecd1a78c2b06f9.\n\nImplemented capture-free arrows as ordinary TypedFunctions and typed HIR FuncRef values. Expression/block bodies, scalar function signatures, contextual parameters at variables/arguments/assignments/returns/assertions, forward named function values, aliases, reassignment, higher-order calls, function returns and generic identity of callbacks execute natively. Function dependency scheduling includes value references and arrow bodies while excluding type-only references. Names use the existing interner and Fx scope/signature tables; known argument/parameter capacities are reserved.\n\nThe shared SSA/type conversion retains HirType::Function on code addresses, including nested function signatures, and represents void results with an empty native return list. Cranelift indirect calls use their actual return type instead of an unconditional integer return, with the result type cached before builder borrowing. Callbacks introduce no closure environment allocation or static frees of code pointers, and native registration leaves the collector disabled.\n\nValidation:\n- cargo test -p zyntax_ts --offline: all 18 tests passed, including pinned conformance, ownership, inference, generics, diagnostics and baseline callbacks.\n- LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX=/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm cargo test -p zyntax_ts --features llvm-backend --offline: all 18 tests passed. The subsequently strengthened callback test also passed with both boolean outcomes, mixed bool/f64 arguments, void/discarded results and returned function pointers. It checks interpreter-only execution first, actual Cranelift installation, mixed Cranelift/LLVM caller-callee states, then actual LLVM installation of all relevant functions.\n- LLVM-enabled compiler regressions: call_convention_agreement (4), closure_body_lowering_tests (7), indirect_call_convention (3) all passed.\n- LLVM-enabled Clippy with all targets, no dependencies and -D warnings passed; cargo fmt and git diff --check passed.\n- Python harness unit tests: 7 passed.\n- Full live TypeScript 6.0.2 / Node oracle comparison: 114 cases across interpreter, baseline and LLVM-selected profiles; 321 PASS, 21 XFAIL, no unexpected outcomes. The runner verified an unchanged executable during comparison (SHA256 401a2b392fdaf0d8e7e233534ee3938fa3cd490013eaa574ea936c5a99d3be11). Profile selection alone does not establish native entry; the separate promotion test verifies that.\n- All 20 newly pinned cases pass, including type/arity/return diagnostics, evaluation order and hot callback calls. The existing pending/closure case passes in all three profiles; KNOWN_FAILURES shrank from 8 entries to 7 and no failures were added.\n- Additional CLI probes of explicitly/contextually void expression bodies preserve console side effects in all three configurations.\n\nRemaining compatibility work stays under parent 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5: lexical captures and closure environments with correct static lifetime management, function object identity/properties, parameter-count adaptation, reference-bearing callback ownership and generic function values. These report Unsupported rather than using incomplete capture lowering or escaping stack environments. Scalar callback work does not enable a collector or claim complete closure compatibility. No execution-speed benchmark was taken.","files":null}]}